{"message_id": "1427a1da-1a6f-4786-b5f3-0035a8ea3f09", "user_id": "da26104c-09f3-41ff-bab8-c6f94c67acad", "created_date": "2023-02-04T22:02:59.885308+00:00", "text": "Help me write some HLS source code to create a FPGA hardware accelerator for a streaming-based linear layer acclerator. Please use the Vitis HLS libaraies including `hls_math.h`, `hls_stream.h`, and `ap_fixed.h`. The inputs to the kernel is one stream for the input vector, and two fixed arrays for the weights and bias. Everything uses fixed point types of `ap_fixed<32,10>`.", "role": "prompter", "lang": "en", "review_count": 3, "review_result": true, "deleted": false, "synthetic": false, "emojis": {"+1": 3, "_skip_reply": 5, "_skip_ranking": 1, "_skip_labeling": 1}, "labels": {"spam": {"value": 0.0, "count": 3}, "lang_mismatch": {"value": 0.0, "count": 3}, "pii": {"value": 0.0, "count": 3}, "not_appropriate": {"value": 0.0, "count": 3}, "hate_speech": {"value": 0.0, "count": 3}, "sexual_content": {"value": 0.0, "count": 3}, "quality": {"value": 0.875, "count": 4}, "toxicity": {"value": 0.1875, "count": 4}, "humor": {"value": 0.0625, "count": 4}, "creativity": {"value": 0.6875, "count": 4}, "violence": {"value": 0.0, "count": 4}}, "detoxify": {"toxicity": 0.0002542664296925068, "severe_toxicity": 3.051218118343968e-05, "obscene": 0.00014470858150161803, "identity_attack": 0.00010915171878878027, "insult": 0.00016080534260254353, "threat": 4.35920046584215e-05, "sexual_explicit": 2.60175766015891e-05}, "message_tree_id": "1427a1da-1a6f-4786-b5f3-0035a8ea3f09", "tree_state": "ready_for_export"}
{"message_id": "a72a7dc4-91f4-4953-a6b3-138e6e1fc3be", "parent_id": "1427a1da-1a6f-4786-b5f3-0035a8ea3f09", "user_id": "df411b83-5030-4708-bf81-0fe9fa1398b2", "created_date": "2023-02-16T03:38:39.194580+00:00", "text": "To create an FPGA hardware accelerator for a streaming-based linear layer, you can use Vitis HLS libraries including hls_math.h, hls_stream.h, and ap_fixed.h. You can start by declaring the input and output streams as well as the weight and bias arrays, using the ap_fixed&#60;32,10&#62; fixed point type. Here is an example of the code:\n\n#include \"hls_math.h\"\n#include \"hls_stream.h\"\n#include \"ap_fixed.h\"\n\ntypedef ap_fixed<32, 10> data_t;\n\nvoid linear_layer_accelerator(hls::stream<data_t> &in_stream,\n                              hls::stream<data_t> &out_stream,\n                              data_t weights[],\n                              data_t bias[]) {\n    // Accelerator body\n}\n\nThe accelerator body should contain a loop to read data from the input stream, calculate the dot product of the input vector and the weights vector, add the bias, and write the output data to the output stream. You can use the HLS math functions to perform the dot product and the addition operations. Here is an example of how to do this:\n\n#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1\n\n// Read data from input stream\ndata_t in_data = in_stream.read();\n\n// Calculate dot product of input vector and weights vector\ndata_t dot_prod = 0;\nfor (int i = 0; i < N_WEIGHTS; i++) {\n    dot_prod += weights[i] * in_data;\n}\n\n// Add bias\ndata_t out_data = dot_prod + bias[0];\n\n// Write output data to output stream\nout_stream.write(out_data);\n\nOnce you have written the body of the accelerator, you can compile it with Vitis HLS to generate the FPGA hardware accelerator.", "role": "assistant", "lang": "en", "review_count": 3, "review_result": true, "deleted": false, "rank": 1, "synthetic": false, "emojis": {"+1": 1, "_skip_labeling": 1}, "labels": {"spam": {"value": 0.0, "count